<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Konjikidō</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1124">1124</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Golden Hall of Chūson-ji</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Konjikidō, or Golden Hall, is a small Buddhist hall at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chuson-ji">Chūson-ji</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiraizumi">Hiraizumi</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, completed in 1124 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-no-kiyohira">Fujiwara no Kiyohira</a>. Its interior and exterior are covered with gold leaf and black lacquer inlaid with mother-of-pearl, and it houses an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amida">Amida</a> Buddha with attendants. The mummified remains of Kiyohira, Motohira and Hidehira lie beneath its altars.</p></div>
